{"kind":"request","text":"Design note: one submission and replica primitive in `packages/client`, built from a declared definition, so that an application's act path is correct by construction.\n\nFiled 2026-11-13 07:43 EST at Hugh's request. On 2026-11-13 Hugh agreed to the six changes and the two decisions below with the words \"yes agree on all\". Every dap fact below is read at main `64112e45`.\n\n## Outcome\n\nAn application author writes a definition, the views, and one typed call for each control. The author decides nothing about keys, retries, late answers, classes of refusal or the return of a draft. dap's client decides each of those in one place, from the definition and the record.\n\n## Why\n\ndap-foldkit M2 built an adapter over `derive` and `client`: a replica, an outbox, an engine and a draft controller, 1374 lines by `wc -l` over seven files in `~/play/dap-foldkit/lib`. Two reviews of it on 2026-11-13 reproduced eleven faults and raised seven findings. Hugh's concern, in his words: \"the application developer be presented with a task that is 'simply correct': that decisions structuring the acts and writing down the application definitions are likely lead to correct behavior. So each of these is a symptom of a structural risk.\"\n\nEach fault has one of two causes. The adapter states again a thing that dap already decides. Or the application author decides a thing that the definition already states. The six changes remove both causes.\n\n## The six changes\n\n1. **A key names the member's request, and not one signing.** The scope gives a key to the first sealed intent of its actor for the life of the scope (`packages/derive/src/judge.ts:218-222`). The adapter mints a new key for each signing and adds a second identity, a handle. A draft adds a third, the submission that its value came from. Three definitions of \"the same request\" disagree: a dismissed notice with unchanged text posts the act twice. The change: a draft takes its key when the member starts it, and every signing of that request carries that key. The scope then records one request one time, whatever the page remembers. `mismatch` then means that the record holds another version of this request.\n2. **`Custody` holds every request.** Planner request `feb01e7d` rules one owner for request custody, and NF-5 `c288c917` moves the browser onto `Custody` (`packages/client/src/custody.ts`). The adapter's outbox is a third implementation of custody. The adapter drops it and uses `Custody`.\n3. **One client, built from the definition.** `declaredHandle(scope, definition)` refuses `definition-mismatch`, and `ScopeHandle.log` checks each page against the summary. The adapter takes the definition in four separate places, and nothing ties them to the scope that it observes. The change: the replica, the judge on it, the typed rows and the typed submit all come from one `DeclaredHandle\u003cD\u003e`.\n4. **Drafts come from the definition.** Today the application writes a codec for each form, calls three draft functions at the right moments, and chooses between \"submit\" and \"retry\". Four of the eleven faults are in that code. The change: the client derives a draft slot from the act's declaration, which is the kind with its `on` item or with its declared `item` fields. One operation checks the values, takes the draft and submits under the draft's key. Returned fields go back to their slot, because the slot is a function of the proposal.\n5. **dap exports what the adapter copies.** A class for each refusal reason (filed separately). One status for signed bytes at a replica's head: recorded, a write with its effects, refused at this head, never effective, not judged. A `mismatch` that names the entry that holds the key. `isItemEffect` (`6fba1c18`).\n6. **The record decides, and an answer is a hint to read.** The adapter orders two inputs by hand: the scope's answers and the fold. The orders that it missed are three of the eleven faults. The change: the client hands an answer to the submission logic only after the replica holds the head that the answer names, which is `judgedAt` for a refusal and the receipt's sequence for an acceptance. Every decision then reads one state.\n\nNot asked: a scope that remembers refusals. The scope keeps its index of acceptance and nothing more.\n\n## Decisions that Hugh made\n\n- An edited retry goes under the same key. `Custody` refuses a second meaning under one key as `conflicting` today, so custody needs a stated rule for it.\n- The replica moves into `packages/client` now, as one primitive with custody. It does not wait for a second application.\n\n## What the note rules on\n\n1. Custody for a key that names a request: successive envelopes under one key. A new signing when the earlier envelope can never take effect. A revised meaning while an earlier envelope can still take effect: the record then holds one of the two, and the page learns which.\n2. Custody reads `refused` as a resolution of `unknown` (`custody.ts:31-34`, `:404`). A refusal speaks for one head. The M0 probe showed bytes that the scope refused at one head and accepted at a later head. With one send lost and a later send refused, custody reads `refused` for an act that the record can still take. The note states which refusals resolve a record, by the class of the reason.\n3. `settle` does nothing for a terminal record, so a refused record whose earlier copy takes effect is never recognized. `ScopeApi.settle` answers `not-found` when the key's accepted intent has another digest (`packages/scope/src/core.ts:806-822`), so settlement cannot report \"another version holds this key\".\n4. A check of custody against a replica: the fold answers \"is this key recorded, and with which intent\" with no request to the service.\n5. A view of custody that a page can hold: the pending requests with their predicted effects, with no signed bytes, published whole on each change.\n6. The resend schedule and the lost-send timeout. `Custody` leaves both to its caller today.\n7. The draft slot for each act kind, derived from `acts[k]`, and the one operation that admits, takes the draft and submits. The rule for an act that opens an item and names no other item.\n8. The delivery of answers after the replica reaches their head, and what the page shows while the replica is behind or the feed is down.\n9. The shape of `mismatch` and of `Settlement` if either gains a member, with the K item if the change is in the kernel.\n10. What stays in a framework adapter. The expected answer: a Model field for the view, one Message, one Subscription, and the typed call.\n11. Its relation to NF-5 `c288c917`: one design, in the order NF-5 first or this first.\n12. Tests: the adapter's generated sequences (400 seeds of 80 steps) extended through draft, edit, dismiss and reload, with the invariant that the record holds each key one time and the page shows each recorded request as recorded.\n\n## Order\n\nThe note is a document and can start now. No build is placed by this request. A build waits for the identifier cutover head to land and for a planner placement under order `fc7576f8`.\n\n## Filing rule\n\nThe request adds no scope kind, no platform definition name, no capability and no tool family. The custody record is a client store record, not a record of a scope. Item 9 can change two answer shapes in `packages/contract`. The note names the K item for that change or leaves the shapes as they are.\n\nSource: `~/play/dap-foldkit/PLAN.md`, draft 3, revision 6, section 13. Related: `6fba1c18`, `38c2ff71`, PG-1 `cb07b59b`, NF-3 `dd0d078a`, NF-5 `c288c917`, `feb01e7d`.","body":{"conditions":"A design note in notes/ rules on each of the twelve items, practice first, with its status line. The note states the order against NF-5 c288c917 and the identifier cutover. Hugh ratifies the note. The planner files one child request for each build step that the note places. The note adds no scope kind, definition name, capability or tool family, or it names the K item.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}